PART E — AI VOCABULARY EXTRACTION FROM IMAGES AND HANDWRITING

16. Improve the Speed of AI-Based Vocabulary Extraction

The current process for extracting German vocabulary from images and handwritten material takes too long.

Investigate the complete extraction pipeline and optimize it without sacrificing accuracy.

Requirements:

* Inspect image preprocessing, OCR or vision-model calls, prompt size, response parsing, retries, and UI state transitions.
* Avoid redundant model calls and unnecessary image transformations.
* Avoid sequential operations when independent operations can safely run in parallel.
* Use an appropriately concise, structured extraction prompt.
* Request structured, machine-readable output when supported by the existing model integration.
* Use bounded timeouts and a sensible retry policy.
* Avoid repeatedly processing the same image or text when a valid result is already available.
* Show meaningful progress feedback while processing.
* Keep the UI responsive during long-running tasks.
* Handle network failures, rate limits, unreadable handwriting, and incomplete model responses gracefully.
* Preserve extraction accuracy, especially for German umlauts, ß, capitalization, noun articles, word forms, and separable verbs.
* Do not silently fabricate vocabulary or claim uncertain handwriting has been recognized with certainty.

Where useful, separate vocabulary recognition from optional enrichment. Prioritize returning the recognized word list promptly rather than making users wait for unnecessary extra explanations.

Do not switch to a different model or remove useful capabilities without first checking the existing implementation and its constraints.

17. Collapse Extraction Details After Successful Extraction

After AI vocabulary extraction from an image or handwritten source completes successfully:

* Automatically collapse the image/input information and explanatory details section.
* Display the extracted vocabulary list as the primary visible content.
* Keep the extracted list easy to review and edit.
* Preserve the ability to reopen the input and explanation section if the user wants to inspect or modify the source.
* Do not collapse the interface before extraction has succeeded.
* If extraction fails, keep the source information visible and display a useful error message.
* Preserve the extracted results when the details section is collapsed or reopened.

The goal is to minimize clutter and make the extracted vocabulary the immediate focus.

