PART F — DUPLICATE AND SIMILAR VOCABULARY MANAGEMENT

18. Add Duplicate and Similar-Word Detection

Add a dedicated tool for finding duplicate and potentially similar vocabulary entries.

This feature must be accessible from:

1. The main vocabulary-management section.
2. The grouped vocabulary-management view or its existing “Group Vocabulary” submenu.

Do not create disconnected implementations for these locations. Reuse a shared component or service where practical.

18.1 Detection

Identify likely duplicates and similar entries using relevant information, including:

* German headword.
* Case-insensitive normalized spelling.
* German capitalization.
* Umlauts and ß, handled carefully so normalization does not create false matches.
* Inflected forms and common spelling variants, where appropriate.
* Persian translation.
* Word category and grammatical information.
* Existing vocabulary identifiers and source information, if available.

Group the results into understandable categories such as:

* Exact duplicates.
* Likely duplicates.
* Similar entries that require manual review.

Do not automatically delete words just because their spellings or meanings are similar. Different German words may have different meanings or grammatical roles.

18.2 User Actions

For each detected group, let the user:

* Keep both entries.
* Delete a selected duplicate.
* Select a canonical entry to retain.
* Merge two or more entries into one final vocabulary record.

Require confirmation before destructive operations.

18.3 AI-Assisted Merge

When the user explicitly chooses to merge entries, use AI to compare their available information and produce the best consolidated vocabulary record.

Compare and reconcile, where applicable:

* German headword and spelling.
* Persian translations.
* Part of speech.
* Article and grammatical gender.
* Plural form.
* Conjugation or declension details.
* Example sentences and their translations.
* Synonyms and related forms.
* Word category.
* Learning notes and other vocabulary metadata.

Merge rules:

* Preserve the most accurate and useful information from both entries.
* Prefer reliable, explicit source information over unsupported guesses.
* Preserve distinct valid meanings rather than collapsing them into one incorrect translation.
* Combine complementary examples when appropriate and remove redundant examples.
* Avoid losing useful grammar or learning information.
* If two entries represent different meanings or grammatical roles, warn the user and recommend keeping separate entries.
* Show the proposed merged record for review before committing the change.
* Allow the user to edit the proposed result before confirming.
* Preserve relevant review progress and historical references when technically possible.
* Update references so that no review history or category entry points to a deleted identifier.
* Handle identifier conflicts safely.
* Save the merged record only after the user confirms.

If AI is unavailable or the merge request fails, provide a safe manual fallback and never delete the original entries automatically.

