Minimum Acceptance Tests

Settings

* All settings load correctly, save correctly, and survive reloads.
* Existing valid user settings are not overwritten.
* Theme, daily limits, order, review intervals, difficult-word handling, audio, timers, goals, and reminders affect real behavior.
* Clearing review history preserves vocabulary.
* Deleting all vocabulary requires the intended confirmations.

Flashcards

* Revealing one card never reveals another card’s answer.
* Examples remain hidden until the corresponding card is revealed.
* Random direction can vary between cards.
* Timers are canceled or reset correctly when navigating between cards.

Search and Import

* Search results inside collapsed groups become immediately visible.
* Clearing search restores normal category behavior.
* Search focus does not unexpectedly zoom the page on mobile.
* Import overwrite works as intended.
* Invalid imports do not corrupt existing data.

AI Extraction

* Extraction is measurably more responsive where avoidable overhead existed.
* The UI remains responsive during extraction.
* Successful extraction collapses the source-details section and focuses on the resulting word list.
* Failed extraction preserves the source details and provides a useful error.

Duplicate Management

* Exact duplicates and likely similar entries are distinguishable.
* Users can safely keep, delete, or merge entries.
* AI-assisted merges preserve useful information from both entries.
* Users can review and edit the merged result before saving.
* Related history and references are handled safely.

Grammar and Conjugation

* The preposition exercise requires two answers.
* Both answers are validated appropriately.
* Regular endings appear blue.
* Actual irregular stem changes appear red.
* Unchanged stem characters remain normally colored.

Word Profiles and Article Colors

* The full vocabulary profile can be opened from all relevant exercise types.
* Closing the popup returns to the exact same exercise state.
* der is blue, feminine singular die is pink, and das is green in the relevant vocabulary-management views.
* Color coding remains legible in both themes.

